  [1m2. Identity and sharing.[0m Who a person is, how a collaborator gets access, and which tasks they can see. At this scale
  this can stay small — likely invite links rather than a full account system — but it's the cycle where "users" stops
  being a word and becomes a table.

  [1m3. Change events.[0m Every mutation records who did what to which task and when. Worth naming separately because it's
  independently useful as an activity log, and because it's the substrate notifications read from.

  [1m4. Notifications.[0m Implicit subscription rules, an inbox with read/unread, and delivery. This is what you asked for,
  and it's last because each of the three above is a hard prerequisite.

  [1mOne piece of notification-thinking belongs in cycle 1[0m, though, and this is the main reason it was worth doing the
  decomposition before designing anything. Cycle 3 is cheap if every task mutation flows through a single chokepoint,
  and expensive if mutations are scattered across a dozen handlers — retrofitting an event log means finding and
  rewriting every write path. So cycle 1 should route writes through one place. That's a structural constraint, not a
  feature: no event log gets built yet, nothing is stored, and there's no cost if you later decide notifications aren't
  worth it. It just keeps the door open.

  I'd resist building anything else for notifications now. Channels, digests, batching, preferences — all of that is
  cheap to add later and expensive to guess at today.
